A||||4||Shiner||'87 Brakes||||5
Z||000000||Shiner||10-06-1999||03:23 AM||||I am doing the brakes on my '87 GT.  The rotors MAY be warped, I do get a pulsing when I am on the brakes, and get some bumping in the steering wheel while braking and turning. Napa told me $10 to grind down a rotor.  What do new ones run?  Should I buy new, or have the old ones ground down?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||10-06-1999||07:07 AM||||Hi,<P>It is OK to have the rotors "turned".  The shop will check them with a micrometer to ensure that they have not worn below the minimum thickness.  If they have then you will have to replace them.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||10-06-1999||08:32 AM||||The new rear rotors for my '87 were $29.99 and the front rotors were $34.99 from Discount Auto Parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||10-06-1999||08:35 PM||||A little off,but J.C.Whitney has Carbon Metallic pads,with two new rotors for $66(a pair).<BR>They only make them for the Grand Am,however,so you'd have to do a little fussing around and do the upgrade/swap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-06-1999||09:32 PM||||Stock rotors with good pads are not bad at all.<P>One step I like to add when I turn rotors is to take a orbital air sander with 220 paper mounted to them while they spin on the lathe. This knocks off the lathe marks left by even the lathes fine setting. Pads seat real quick that way.||||reg||1
